I sent the report to the coordinator. It's in commit `5c1defe40`, pushed to `main` and to `claude/worker-brief-fable-vnnuv8`.

- **Route B(b)'s precondition does not hold.** Compiled code fixes a generic's static argument at the call site only when nothing more specific can apply at run time. When a call is dispatched, each generic arm takes its argument from the run-time value, and that value records only the last-listed instantiation.
- **What the probes printed.** Through `Tag[ZZ32]` and `Tag[String]` variables holding `Both`, every call gave the right answer: `Marker[ZZ32]` and `Marker[String]`. Through an `Any` variable holding `Both`, the call threw `ClassCastException`. A set of two generics, `grab` on `Tag` and `grab` on `Sub`, is accepted by both B(a) and B(b) and has no non-generic specialisation. It still type-checks and then throws "`Marker⟦Red⟧` cannot be cast to `Marker⟦Blue⟧`". So route B also needs the dispatcher to use the call site's static arguments; I did not measure how big that change is.
- **Route C's overload check.** It is a 28-line guard in `OverloadingOracle.satisfiesReturnTypeRule`. On the interpreter's library it refuses 69 pairs in `FortressLibrary.fsi`, 6 of which the stock return type rule already refuses. Limited to generics whose return type mentions the parameter, it refuses 54. Every refused pair is the tower or a comparison type implementing an exempted trait's own functional methods; no top-level pair is refused.
- **Symbolic operators are not in that count.** The compiled overloading checker never examines overloads of `=`, `<`, `+`, `-` and the rest. Counted from the text, they add at most 57 more pairs.
- **Two separate code generator defects turned up; neither involves multiple instantiation.** A generic whose return type mentions `X` always throws when it is reached through a less specific declaration. A `ZZ32` instantiation made at run time gets a different class name from the static one, so it throws even for an object with one instantiation.

Still open:
- whether route C needs the same dispatcher change for generic-beside-generic sets;
- how the interpreter handles the `Sub`/`Tag` program (not run);
- the symbolic-operator pairs, which are counted from the text only.

Neither defect, nor the checker skipping symbolic operators, is in the gap ledger yet.

Everything is in `explorations/reviews/mie-probes/`; the report is `dispatch-and-route-c.md`. Alongside it:
- the probe programs and their `*.compiled.txt` captures;
- `javap-excerpts.txt`;
- `routeC-library.txt` and `routeC-textcount.txt`;
- `shadow/shadow.patch`;
- `dispatch-run.sh`, which reproduces it all.
